  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Silver Empowerment shows an early but meaningful impact profile: it has been downloaded 2,448 times, cited 4 times, and linked to an active policy-relevant conversation on empowerment and reduced inequalities in later life. The record also shows funded support from KU Leuven, the be.Source KU Leuven Chair on empowerment of underprivileged elderly, and HIVA \u2013 KU Leuven, suggesting the book sits within an established research and practice agenda.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Silver Empowerment, published by Leuven University Press in 2023, advances the idea of \u201csilver empowerment\u201d as a way to rethink ageing through strengths, dignity, inclusion, and social connection rather than decline alone. The book is openly available in hybrid form and has already attracted sustained use, with OAPEN reporting 2,448 downloads. That level of access-based uptake indicates that the book is reaching readers beyond its immediate publication context.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Scholarly citation evidence is still modest, with 4 citations recorded in OpenAlex, so the book\u2019s citation footprint should be read as an emerging signal rather than a mature one. Even so, the citing literature shows that its framing is being taken up in adjacent work on loneliness, psychosocial well-being in older age, and psychological empowerment. The strongest concentration appears in gerontology and social work, including repeated citation in the Journal of Gerontological Social Work, which suggests the book is informing ongoing conceptual and measurement work in this area.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation landscape is also notable for its thematic alignment with public policy and applied practice. Recorded citing works connect the book to health disparities, community health and development, and implementation-oriented health policy research, as well as a chapter on social isolation and bio-psychosocial well-being in later life. This pattern fits the book\u2019s core message: empowerment in ageing is being treated not only as a scholarly concept, but as a practical lens for designing more inclusive support systems.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The broader record reinforces that interpretation. The book is associated with the SDG theme of Reduced inequalities, and the funding trail points to institutional investment in empowerment for underserved older adults. Taken together, the evidence suggests that Silver Empowerment is already serving as a reference point for researchers and practitioners working on ageing, loneliness, dignity, and inclusion, with its influence currently strongest in applied gerontology and education-oriented discourse.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Because the citation count is still low, the safest reading is that this is an emerging and interdisciplinary impact story: strong on downloads and conceptually aligned with active research and policy conversations, with scholarly uptake beginning to build through a small but relevant set of citing works.</p>",
